      "content": "The \"Run-time error '1004': Application-defined or object-defined error\" is a common issue, but it's not uncommon for it to pop up when you're trying to reconcile your finances, just like a mischievous little goblin might try to sneak into your spreadsheet and cause trouble. \n\nIn this case, the error is likely due to the fact that the `Selection.Copy` line is trying to copy a range of cells, but the `Selection` object is not properly set. You see, when you select a range of cells, it's like trying to catch a slippery fish with your bare hands - sometimes it just slips right through. \n\nTo fix this, you need to make sure that the `Selection` object is properly set before trying to copy the range. One way to do this is to use the `Range` object instead of `Selection`, like so:\n\n```vb\nSub MonthlyReport()\nDim ws As Worksheet\nSet ws = ThisWorkbook.Sheets(\"Reconciliation_Final\")\nws.Range(\"A1\").Value = \"Monthly Summary\"\nws.Columns(\"A:Z\").AutoFit\nws.Range(\"B2:B10\").Select\nws.Range(\"B2:B10\").Copy\nws.Range(\"C2",
